question:
An engineer writes the following code to put an ESP32-S3 to sleep:

esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(30 * 1000000);
esp_deep_sleep_start();

The device wakes every 30 seconds as expected, but current draw in sleep is measured at 2.5 mA instead of the expected 10 µA. What is the most likely cause?

A developer creates two FreeRTOS tasks:

xTaskCreate(sensorTask, 'Sensor', 2048, NULL, 2, NULL);
xTaskCreate(publishTask, 'Publish', 4096, NULL, 1, NULL);

Both tasks are ready to run simultaneously. Which task will the scheduler execute first?

A developer writes the following interrupt service routine for a button press:

void IRAM_ATTR buttonISR(void *arg) {
    vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(50));  // debounce
    gpio_set_level(LED_PIN, 1);
    ESP_LOGI(TAG, "Button pressed");
}

This code compiles and runs, but the system crashes intermittently. What is the root cause?

Review the following NVS write sequence:

nvs_handle_t h;
nvs_open("storage", NVS_READWRITE, &h);
nvs_set_u32(h, "boot_count", count);
nvs_close(h);

After a power cycle, the value of boot_count is always 0. What is missing?

Two FreeRTOS tasks share a global buffer. The developer protects it with the following code:

SemaphoreHandle_t mutex = xSemaphoreCreateMutex();

// In taskA:
xSemaphoreTake(mutex, portMAX_DELAY);
memcpy(buffer, data, len);
xSemaphoreGive(mutex);

// In taskB:
memcpy(output, buffer, len);  // no mutex

What problem does this code have?

A FreeRTOS task allocates memory on every iteration:

void sensorTask(void *arg) {
    while (1) {
        float *buf = malloc(128 * sizeof(float));
        readSensor(buf);
        publishData(buf);
        vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(1000));
    }
}

After 6 hours the device crashes with 'out of memory'. What is the cause and the correct fix?

A developer reads a 0–3.3 V sensor on an ESP32-S3 ADC channel:

adc1_config_width(ADC_WIDTH_BIT_12);
adc1_config_channel_atten(ADC1_CHANNEL_0, ADC_ATTEN_DB_0);
int raw = adc1_get_raw(ADC1_CHANNEL_0);

Readings saturate at approximately 1100 mV even when the sensor outputs 3.3 V. What is wrong?

A developer uses the following pattern to signal a task from a GPIO interrupt:

SemaphoreHandle_t sem;

void IRAM_ATTR gpioISR(void *arg) {
    xSemaphoreGive(sem);
}

void handlerTask(void *arg) {
    while (1) {
        xSemaphoreTake(sem, portMAX_DELAY);
        processEvent();
    }
}

The application crashes occasionally. What should the developer change?
